<div class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">The electrolyte system</p>
    <h2>Four principal ions, plus citrate chemistry</h2>
    <p>Sodium chloride, sodium bicarbonate and potassium chloride dissociate in water and provide the principal ions emphasized throughout our formula story: <strong>sodium, potassium, chloride and bicarbonate</strong>. Citric acid participates in acid-base equilibria and can contribute citrate-containing ionic species depending on the pH of the prepared drink.</p>
    <p>We describe five highlighted electrolyte species in the prepared formula: sodium, potassium, chloride, bicarbonate and citrate. This wording describes the formula’s solution chemistry and should not be interpreted as a formal claim that all five are essential dietary minerals under nutrition-reference systems.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Formula accuracy</span>
    <h3>No magnesium in the current formula.</h3>
    <p>Magnesium is an important physiological electrolyte, but it is not listed as an ingredient in the current Best Hydrate Lemonade formula. We do not include it in our product ingredient panel or imply that it is present.</p>
  </aside>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Glucose</p>
    <h2>Included for a physiological reason, not to make a very sweet drink</h2>
    <p>Glucose has a direct role in intestinal sodium-glucose cotransport through SGLT1. That mechanism is foundational to oral rehydration science. The broader lesson is not that every hydration drink should contain large amounts of sugar, but that carbohydrate concentration and electrolyte composition can be designed intentionally for the intended use.</p>
    <p>Best Hydrate’s public product description positions the formula as “not too sweet.” The exact preparation instructions and serving composition on the product label should remain the controlling source for how the product is mixed and used.</p>
    <a class="bhd-page__button" href="/pages/glucose-water-absorption">Explore glucose &amp; water absorption</a>
  </div>
</section>

